\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Two mass directions and a choice of depth dependence}
\date{2026-11-28}

\p{The projector images of \ref{fdip-01CL} give
##{\Phi_{\lambda,k}(\bbI)=\lambda^{-1}\bbI,
\qquad
\Phi_{\lambda,k}(P_k^{(k)})=\lambda^{-1}P_{k-1}^{(k-1)}.}
Thus the identity family and the family of top projectors have the
same [scaling eigenvalue](fdip-01CQ), although the first changes
every precision eigenvalue and the second changes only the constant
field. For #{0<\lambda<1} both directions are relevant.}

\p{Allowing an arbitrary coefficient at each depth produces more
scaling families. For any #{\varepsilon>0}, set
#{R_k=(\lambda\varepsilon)^k\bbI}. Then
##{\Phi_{\lambda,k}(R_k)
=\lambda^{-1}(\lambda\varepsilon)^k\bbI
=\varepsilon R_{k-1}.}
Consequently the unrestricted families have every positive scaling
eigenvalue. A list of named directions does not exhaust these families.}
